    },
    {
      "id": "pWdGg",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>As</mi></math>",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "qYEXi",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Bi</mi></math>",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>In group <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mmultiscripts><mo>]</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>&#8594;</mo></math>&nbsp;Non-metal</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mmultiscripts><mo>]</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>Sb</mi><mi>As</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>&#8594;</mo></math>&nbsp;Metalloid</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>Bi</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>&#8594;</mo></math>&nbsp;Metal</p><p>Hydrides of group 15 elements are</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>PH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>AsH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SbH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BiH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p><p>In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>, hydrogen atom gets partial positive charge due to less electronegativity. But in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BiH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>, hydrogen atom gets partial negative charge because hydrogen is more electronegative than bismuth.</p><p>i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BiH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> is a strong reducing agent than others because we know that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math> is a strong reducing agent.</p>",
